## From ID to a list of numbers

A token ID is just a label: 3797 for *" cat"* says nothing about cats. So the first thing GPT-2 does
with each token is look up a list of 768 numbers for it, called an <GlossaryPopover term="embedding">embedding</GlossaryPopover> or a vector. Step through to see the
running example become vectors. (Only the first 48 numbers are drawn; striped cells are negative.)

<TokenToVector />

Nobody chose these numbers. They start out random and are adjusted during training, which Part III
explains. After training, tokens that are used in similar ways end up with similar
lists.<Cite id="radford2019" />

## Similar meanings, nearby points

A list of numbers can be read as a position. Two numbers place a point on a flat map; 384 or 768
numbers place it in a space with that many directions. Nearby points have similar lists, and similar
lists turn out to mean similar things.

The map below uses all-MiniLM-L6-v2, a small model built for comparing meanings, which turns any
text into 384 numbers.<Cite id="minilm-model-card" /> Pick a word to see its nearest neighbors.

<EmbeddingMap />

The map is a flattened view. Squashing 384 numbers onto a page loses most of the detail, so a few
neighbors may look far apart on the map. The lists of neighbors and similarity scores use all 384
numbers.

## Directions mean something too

Tick *Show king − man + woman*. Taking the vector for *king*, subtracting *man*, and adding *woman*
gives a list of numbers whose nearest word on this map, comparing all 384 numbers, is *queen*. That
works because the step from *man* to *woman* partly lines up with the step from *king* to *queen*.
Squashed onto the flat map, the two steps don't look alike: the map keeps too little of the detail.
This famous example was first shown in
2013 with word vectors from a model called word2vec.<Cite id="mikolov2013" /> It doesn't work for
every analogy, and on this map it only has a few dozen words to choose from, but it shows that
directions in the space can carry meaning.

<RecapCard>

- Each token is turned into a vector: a list of numbers (768 for GPT-2 small).
- The numbers are learned, and tokens used in similar ways end up with similar vectors.
- Similar vectors are nearby points, and some directions carry meaning, like *man* to *woman*.

</RecapCard>

**Next question:** the vectors are learned, but learned by what? What does the model do with all
these numbers?
